## 4. Selection and sizing

- The bar profile, including bends and joints, because the sleeve has to recover around them.
- Phase spacing required after insulation, which is the design driver in most panel rebuilds.
- Whether the assembly has to meet a tracking or CTI requirement, which decides the compound.
- Copper or aluminium bar, and the surface preparation that goes with each.
- Whether the build is tested as an assembly, in which case the clearance rules of the assembly standard govern.

## 5. Standards commonly specified

- IEC 61439-1, low voltage switchgear and controlgear assemblies, for clearance and creepage practice.
- IEC 60664-1, insulation coordination.
- IEC 60112, determination of comparative tracking index.
- ASTM D2303, inclined plane tracking and erosion.
- UL 94, flammability of the polymer.

Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Deburr the bar and clean it before sleeving. An edge under a recovered sleeve is a stress point.
- Recover from the centre of the bar outwards so air is driven to the ends.
- Do not cut the sleeve back to a sharp edge at a bend. Leave the specified overlap onto the plain bar.
- Check the recovered wall at bends, since recovery thins the wall slightly where it stretches.
- Torque the connections to specification after sleeving, since the sleeve changes the surface under the fastener.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Flashover between phases | Phase clearance below the requirement, with the sleeve assumed to carry more than it does | Calculate clearance with the sleeved bar profile and apply the assembly standard, not a rule of thumb. |
| Tracking and erosion in service | Compound not matched to polluted or humid service | Select an anti-tracking grade with a declared CTI for the environment. |
| Sleeve split at a bend | Bar not deburred, or recovery carried out with too much local heat | Deburr and clean the bar, and recover with even heat around the circumference. |
| Loose connection after sleeving | Fastener torqued before the sleeve was fully recovered, or surface change not accounted for | Complete recovery first, then torque to specification. |

## 8. Typical applications

- Switchgear and controlgear panel builds and rebuilds.
- Substation and transformer connections using insulated bar.
- Industrial distribution boards where phase spacing is tight.
- Export panel builders who have to demonstrate clearance compliance.

